le Democratic turnout problems in North Carolina could narrow the margin in the final days.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's Senate race is an open contest after Thom Tillis decided not to run again, pitting former Governor Roy Cooper against Republican Michael Whatley. Every poll released in October, including surveys from Republican-leaning firms, shows Cooper ahead, with margins ranging from eight to fourteen points. Cooper has also raised roughly three and a half times Whatley's total and enters the final week with about ten times his cash on hand.\n\nThe state's narrow Republican lean is the main counterweight, and a double-digit Cooper win would be a strong showing in a state that has tilted slightly red in recent cycles. Still, prediction markets put Democratic odds near 97 to 98 percent, and eleven ratings outlets all place the race on the Democratic side of the ledger, most calling it Likely Democratic. Vice President Vance's late rally for Whatley underscores Republican efforts, but the accumulated evidence points to Cooper as a clear favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.93, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9375, "margin": 8.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9346, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9264, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9322, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.939, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9386, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9577, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9557, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9557, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9528, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.9512, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.9379, "margin"